Preparation of 4 $\mathrm{(}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}C_{81}\mathrm{H}_{78}\mathrm{O}_{34}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC})_{4}(\mathrm{BCMTC})_{0.5})$ . MOF 4 was prepared using a modified synthetic route.96 Crystals of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC)}_{4}$ (30 mg, $16~\mathrm{\upmumol}_{,\cdot}$ ) were added to solution of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BCMTC}$ $(42~\mathrm{mg},0.12~\mathrm{mmol})$ in $4~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF in a one-dram vial. The vial was placed in a preheated oven at $75~^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ then cooled to room temperature over two hours. Light tan crystals of 4 ( $35~\mathrm{mg}$ , $14\mathrm{\:\upmumol}$ ) were isolated in $64\%$ yield. FTIR (neat, $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ): 3450, 2935, 2855, 1657, 1586, 1544, 149,5 1411, 1384, 1255, 1208, 1139, 1091. The PXRD pattern of 4 is shown in Figure S18. The $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra of digested 4 is shown in Figure S19.
UV-vis, diffuse reflectance, and fluorescence spectroscopies. Steady-state emission spectra were acquired on an Edinburgh FS5 fluorescence spectrometer equipped with a $150\mathrm{~W~}$ Continuous Wave Xenon Lamp source for excitation. Emission measurements on solid samples were collected on the powders of the appropriate materials placed inside a $0.5~\mathrm{mm}$ quartz sample holder using the front-facing module. The Ocean Optics JAZ spectrometer was also used for absorbance and diffuse reflectance measurements. An Ocean Optics ISP-REF integrating sphere was connected the spectrometer using a $450~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ SMA fiber optic cable. A $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ long pass filter was placed between an $8.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ quartz sample cell with cover and the integrating sphere to filter any UV light from the internal tungsten-halogen lamp. An Ocean Optics WS-1 Spectralon $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ reflectance standard was placed on the sample cell throughout the measurements. A mounted high-powered LED (M365L2, Thorlabs), $\lambda=365~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , was used for in situ irradiation of the samples. Before time-resolved UV-vis and diffuse reflectance measurements, the sample background was subtracted to obtain the spectra corresponding to photophysical behavior of the photochromic derivatives.
$^{13}\mathbf{C}$ CP-MAS NMR Spectroscopy. Solid-state NMR spectra $^{\cdot13}\mathrm{C}$ CP-MAS) were collected on a Bruker Avance III-HD ${500}~\mathrm{MHz}$ spectrometer fitted with a $1.9\ \mathrm{mm\MAS}$ probe. $^{13}\mathrm{C}\{^{1}\mathrm{H}\}$ CP-MAS NMR spectra $(125.79~\mathrm{MHz})$ were collected at ambient temperature with a sample rotation rate of $20~\mathrm{kHz}$ . For cross polarization, $2.0~\mathrm{ms}$ contact time with linear ramping on the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ channel and
$62.5~\mathrm{{kHz}}$ field on the $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ channel were used. $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ dipolar decoupling was performed with SPINAL64 modulation and $147~\mathrm{kHz}$ field strength. Free induction decays (2048–5000 transients) were collected with a 27 ms acquisition time over a $400~\mathrm{ppm}$ spectra width with a relaxation delay of $2.0~\mathrm{s}$ .
Other Physical Measurements. FTIR spectra were obtained on a Perkin-Elmer Spectrum 100. NMR spectra were collected on Bruker Avance III-HD 300 and Bruker Avance III $400~\mathrm{{MHz}}$ NMR spectrometers. $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra were referenced to natural abundance $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ peaks and residual $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ peaks of deuterated solvents, respectively. Powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction patterns were recorded on a Rigaku Miniflex II diffractometer with accelerating voltage and current of $30~\mathrm{kV}$ and $15~\mathrm{mA}$ , respectively. The Waters QTOF-I quadrupole time-of-flight and Thermo Scientific Orbitrap Velos Pro mass-spectrometers were used to record the mass-spectra of the prepared compounds.
